-The projected $\dot q$, not the unprojected delayed growth at the quality ceiling, enters this identity. Every integrand is in gha\,yr$^{-1}$; every integral is in gha. The fast-yield channel is split into $A_fe^{-\alpha D}\dot T_b$ (technology) and $-\alpha A_fb_f\dot D$ (debt). Debt growth contributes negatively to yield, whereas debt decay contributes positively. A signed time integral can hide such within-run reversals and should not be read as a peak-debt metric.
-
-For each trajectory, code evaluates all five continuous-RHS integrands independently at every saved node and integrates with the trapezoidal rule. The endpoint change is separately computed from $B(T)-B(0)$. The signed residual is $\varepsilon_T=B(T)-B(0)-\sum_j I_j$. No integral is inferred as a balancing remainder, rescaled, or corrected to force closure. This differs from a telescoping discrete product decomposition, which could close exactly by construction. The retained first-order Euler convention and the quality-cap kink imply finite-step discrepancies; the refinement results below are numerical checks, not a general convergence theorem.
-
-\subsection{Selected existing cases and implementation}
-
-Only four previously specified cases are budgeted. F100 uses $A_{c0}=1.5$\,ha, $P_0=1.5$\,cap, frozen $E=0.825$\,gha, $\chi_r=0$, and $T=100$\,yr. R400 uses the existing recovery case $A_{c0}=1.0$\,ha, $P_0=2.0$\,cap, $E=eP$, $\chi_r=0.1$\,gha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$, and $T=400$\,yr. N250 and W250 are the no-wave/wave pair in S8 at $A_{c0}=0.9$\,ha, $P_0=1.0$\,cap, $E=eP$, $\chi_r=0.1$\,gha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$, and $T=250$\,yr. All share $A_{f0}=0.6$\,ha, $q_0=0.9$, $D_0=0$, $A_{tot}=2.5$\,ha, and the S8 baseline coefficients and delays. Technology is off except in W250. F100 still integrates the population equation but does not feed it into its fixed demand. No controller, new history, new delay setting, new horizon, or empirical physical-stock series is introduced.
-
-The supplied \texttt{code/compute\_c13.py} is an independent reconstruction compatible with the C12 equations and numerical description, not recovered original author code. At each Euler step it reads the old current state, evaluates the wave and delayed inputs at the right-end time, recomputes current supply on that old state with the right-end wave, and then updates. The post-update supply is stored for delayed-history lookup. Linear interpolation is used for the delayed states. Pre-run quality is constant at $0.9$; pre-run $B$ is the constant no-wave initial value, including in the wave pair as specified in S8. The projected quality velocity is followed by finite-step clipping. Nonnegative guards are applied to $A_f,P,D$, the $A_c=0.05$\,ha floor is guarded, and $A_r=A_{tot}-A_f-A_c$ is reconstructed and checked, not projected. The inactive optional $A_f$ ceiling and alternative restoration gates are not introduced.
-
-At $\Delta t=0.05$\,yr, the reconstruction gives the S8 $P_0=1.0$ debt peaks $0.0081259288$\,gha\,yr at $86.15$\,yr and $0.0202046739$\,gha\,yr at $115.60$\,yr, matching the printed six-decimal values. Figure~\ref{fig:si_wave_debt} is freshly rendered from these matched runs. The other seven supplied figures are inherited assets with explicit file-level provenance; they were not regenerated or comprehensively revalidated in this revision. The package includes all figure dependencies for compilation, but this selected M1/M2 reconstruction does not rerun all legacy analyses.
